Both files are implemented and `python3 test_impl.py` prints **ALL PASS**. The stress section took 2.8s. I didn't touch `test_impl.py`.

- **`rle.py`**
  - `encode` writes each run of the same character as the character followed by its count, so `'aaabbc'` becomes `'a3b2c1'`. A single character still gets a count of 1, and an empty string returns `''`.
  - `decode` reverses this by reading each character followed by its count, which can be more than one digit.
- **`intervals.py`**: `merge` sorts the intervals by start, then joins any interval that overlaps or touches the previous one. So `[1,2],[2,3]` becomes `[1,3]`.

One limitation: the encoding can't be reversed correctly if the original string contains digits. For example, `encode("11")` gives `"12"`, which `decode` reads as "1" once and returns `"1"`. The docstring format has this problem built in, and none of the tests use digits.
